To access the fourth element of a linked list in Java, you need to traverse the list starting from the head node. Since linked lists do not support random access like arrays, you must iterate through the nodes one by one until you reach the fourth position.

Here is a simple example using a custom `Node` class and a `LinkedList` class:

```java
class Node {
    int data;
    Node next;

    Node(int data) {
        this.data = data;
        this.next = null;
    }
}

public class LinkedList {
    private Node head;

    public void add(int data) {
        Node newNode = new Node(data);
        if (head == null) {
            head = newNode;
        } else {
            Node current = head;
            while (current.next != null) {
                current = current.next;
            }
            current.next = newNode;
        }
    }

    public int getFourthElement() {
        if (head == null) {
            throw new NoSuchElementException("List is empty");
        }

        Node current = head;
        int count = 1;

        // Traverse until we reach the 4th element
        while (current != null && count < 4) {
            current = current.next;
            count++;
        }

        if (current == null) {
            throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException("List has fewer than 4 elements");
        }

        return current.data;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LinkedList list = new LinkedList();
        list.add(10);
        list.add(20);
        list.add(30);
        list.add(40);
        list.add(50);

        try {
            int fourth = list.getFourthElement();
            System.out.println("The fourth element is: " + fourth); // Output: 40
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Points:
- Start at the `head` node.
- Move to the `next` node repeatedly until you've counted 4 nodes.
- If the list has fewer than 4 elements, handle the exception appropriately.
- This approach has a time complexity of O(n) in the worst case
